Quinovo is your long-term memory for branding decisions. Use its MCP tools so every repo you touch stays consistent across sessions.

After generating branding for a repo, call the quinovo `remember` tool with:

- the repo's path and name
- the chosen README structure decisions (badges used, demo command recorded, sections included)
- any name or one-liner you proposed and which was picked
- anything the user rejected, and why

Keep the memory factual and short — future-you reads this, not a human.

Before rebranding or updating a repo you may have touched before, call quinovo `search_around` for prior decisions about that repo. If prior decisions exist, follow them unless the user explicitly overrides — consistency across a portfolio is the point. If they conflict with the current request, surface the conflict in one sentence and ask which wins.

If the quinovo server is unreachable, note it once and continue without memory — never block presentation work on memory.
